"El Zein-Mortada Saturated Packing Refutation | trureturing (https://www.trureturing.org/research/elzein-mortada-saturated-packing-conjecture-refutation/)\nciteturn10search0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; El Zein and Mortada, arXiv:2603.25113v1, Conjecture 3, printed page 27, state: \"Every 2-saturated subcubic graph is (1, 1, 2)-packing colorable.\" ... `theorem`; Tier 1 recent named external conjecture, preregistered in issue 8799 before the proof probe.\n\n# El Zein-Mortada Saturated Packing Refutation\n\nLiterature status: not rechecked Repository record: refuted\n\nResolution source: D5/S3/Combinatorics/Graph/ElZeinMortadaSaturatedPackingRefutation.result10.48550/arXiv.2603.25113Source dossierRelease 36a49830636b\n\nProblem\n\n## The question\n\nEl Zein and Mortada, arXiv:2603.25113v1, Conjecture 3, printed page 27, state: \"Every 2-saturated subcubic graph is (1, 1, 2)-packing colorable.\" A subcubic graph has maximum degree at most three. It is 2-saturated when each degree-three vertex has at most two degree-three neighbors. A `(1,1,2)`-packing coloring partitions the vertices into two independent classes and one class whose distinct vertices have distance greater than two. The printed conjecture contains no local-girth hypothesis.\n\nMotivation\n\n## Our foothold\n\nThe frozen theorem `D5/S3/Combinatorics/Graph/ElZeinMortadaSaturatedPackingRefutation.result` settles the printed universal statement by an exact seven-vertex counterexample. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nImpact of local girth on the S-packing coloring of k-saturated subcubic graphs | alphaXiv (https://www.alphaxiv.org/ko/abs/2603.25113)\nciteturn10search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 7 months ago; Crawled: 2 weeks ago; Brešar, Kuenzel, and Rall [Discrete Math. 348(8) (2025),~114477] proved that every claw-free cubic graph is $(1,1,2,2)$(1,1,2,2)-packing colorable, confirming the conjecture for this family. ... @misc{zein2026impactlocalgirthspacking, title={Impact of local girth on the S-packing coloring of k-saturated subcubic graphs}, author={Ayman El Zein and Maidoun Mortada}, year={2026}, eprint={2603.25113}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25113}, }\nSubmitted 26 Mar 2026\n\n# Impact of local girth on the S-packing coloring of k-saturated subcubic graphs\n\nAE\n\nAyman El Zein\n\nMM\n\nMaidoun Mortada\n\n## Abstract\n\nFor a non-decreasing sequence $S=(s_1,s_2,\\dots,s_k)$S=(s 1​,s 2​,…,s k​), an $S$S-packing coloring of a graph $G$G is a vertex coloring using the colors $s_1,s_2,\\dots,s_k$s 1​,s 2​,…,s k​ such that any two vertices assigned the same color $s_i$s i​ are at distance greater than $s_i$s i​. A subcubic graph is said to be $k$k-saturated, for $0\\le k\\le3$0≤k≤3, if every vertex of degree 3 is adjacent to at most $k$k vertices of degree~3. The local girth of a vertex is the length of the smallest cycle containing it. Brešar, Kuenzel, and Rall [Discrete Math. 348(8) (2025),~114477] proved that every claw-free cubic graph is $(1,1,2,2)$(1,1,2,2)-packing colorable, confirming the conjecture for this family. Equivalently, a claw-free cubic graph is one in which each $3$3-vertex has local girth~3. Motivated by this observation and by recent progress on $S$S-packing colorings of $k$k-saturated subcubic graphs, we study the influence of local girth on their $S$S-packing colorability. We establish a series of results describing how the parameters of saturation and local girth jointly determine the admissible $S$S-packing sequences. Sharpness is verified through explicit constructions, and several open problems are posed to delineate the remaining cases.\n\n## BibTeX\n\nCopy\n\n@misc{zein2026impactlocalgirthspacking, title={Impact of local girth on the S-packing coloring of k-saturated subcubic graphs}, author={Ayman El Zein and Maidoun Mortada}, year={2026}, eprint={2603.25113}, archivePrefix={arXiv}, primaryClass={math.CO}, url={https://arxiv.org/abs/2603.25113}, }--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nPacking chromatic number conjecture for 0-saturated subcubic graphs | MathDB (https://mathdb.com/p/373854/packing-chromatic-number-conjecture-for-0-saturated-subcubic)\nciteturn10search2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 weeks ago; # Packing chromatic number conjecture for 0-saturated subcubic graphs ... Ayman El Zein and Maidoun Mortada, “Impact of local girth on the S-packing coloring of k-saturated subcubic graphs”, arXiv:2603.25113 (2026).\n\n# Packing chromatic number conjecture for 0-saturated subcubic graphs\n\nAbout 10 years old · traced to 2016\n\nLet $G$G be a $0$0-saturated subcubic graph, and let $g_3(G)$g 3​(G) denote its local girth parameter. Packing chromatic number conjecture. If\n\n$$g_3(G)\\leq 4,$$g 3​(G)≤4,\n\nthen\n\n$$\\chi_{\\rho}(G)\\leq 4.$$χ ρ​(G)≤4.\n\nThe paper proves the corresponding upper bound $\\chi_{\\rho}(G)\\leq 6$χ ρ​(G)≤6 and reports no example with packing chromatic number at least $5$5; the claimed improvement to $4$4 remains open.\n\nReferences\n\n## Primary source\n\nAyman El Zein and Maidoun Mortada, “Impact of local girth on the S-packing coloring of k-saturated subcubic graphs”, arXiv:2603.25113 (2026).\n\n## Additional references\n\n2 papers in this index state this conjecture (2016–2026). The statement above is taken from the most recent of them; the others are arXiv:1608.05573.\n\ngraph-coloringpacking-coloringsubcubic-graphs\n\nPosted 2 months ago\n\n## Progress summary\n\nRefresh Never refreshed\n\nNothing recorded yet. Refresh searches the literature and the public web for attempts on this problem, and writes the first summary here.\n\n## Solutions 0\n\n[Button: Post a solution]\n\n[Button: Solve with an agent]\n\nNo solutions have been posted yet.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nFive-color packing coloring conjecture for 2-saturated subcubic graphs | MathDB (https:/